// If you run the Glos compiler with no command line arguments, it will attempt to build the
// module in the current working directory.
//
// What is a module? It is a folder containing '*.glos' files.

// For this example, you can change your current directory inside this folder, and run glos.
//
//     $ glos
//
// Or
//
//     $ glos .
//
// You can pass the '-r' flag if you want. Just don't pass an input file.
//
// Another thing you can do, is stay in any other directory, and run glos providing the path
// to this folder as the input.
//
// For example if you are in the root of this repo:
//
//     $ glos how_to/14_modules
//
// Again, you can pass the '-r' flag if you want. That is irrelevant to this discussion.

main :: () {
    // The function foo() is not defined here. However since you executed glos on the entire
    // folder, therefore it will find its definition in the file 'foo.glos'.
    //
    // Since 'main.glos' and 'foo.glos' are present in the same folder, they are part of the
    // same module, and shares definitions.
    foo()

    // If you execute glos as such:
    //
    //     glos -r main.glos
    //
    // Then 'foo()' will be defined, since the root module will contain only the file 'main.glos'
    // and not the entire folder.

    // Now we are going to import a module. As you can see, there is a subfolder 'bar' inside
    // this directory. We are going to import that.
    //
    // Note: While it is allowed to pass a file as the input to the compiler from the CLI, you
    //       can NOT import a file. You can only import folders.

    bar :: #import "bar"
    bar.bar()

    // The following line is an error, since 'bar_impl' is not exported from module "bar"
    // bar.bar_impl()

    // You don't have to define an import as a constant to use it. If you want you can just use
    // it directly.
    #import "bar".more()
    // However it's kinda ugly, so the constant definition is visually preferred.

    // Now, let's talk about unqualified imports
    #import "nice"
    // That's it. Just import it as a statement.

    nice() // Imported from module "nice"

    // As a general rule of thumb though, qualified imports are always preferred over unqualified
    // imports. It makes reasoning about code much easier. Also it prevents name collisons.

    // If a name was defined in the current lexical scope which collides with an unqualified import,
    // then the one defined in the scope will be used, not the import one.

    // For example, the module "nice" defines a function 'shadowed' that prints 420. However we shall
    // shadow that with our own definition of 'shadowed'
    shadowed()

    shadowed :: () {
        println("Get rekt noob")
        // If you comment this function out, then it will print 420. Try it out!
    }

    // However, if the collision occurs with a definition that is also from an unqualified import,
    // then the compiler will throw a redefinition error.
    //
    // Uncomment this line and see the compiler errors. They are quite descriptive, showing where
    // the names were imported from, to provide a good developer experience.
    // #import "collision"
}

// Check out the files in the folder "bar" to get an idea about the '#private' directive.

// You can apply '#private' to an entire 'extern' block. It would do exactly what you expect.
#private extern {
    foo :: ()
    bar :: ()
}

// If the '#private ...' directive is used inside a '#if' branch, then it will only apply to that
// branch, not the other ones, and not outside.
